content.text:
The review found no crash or soundness bug. A Verified verdict on an over-narrowed domain is reported VACUOUS by the probe, not HELD. I read the PR diff and the surrounding code in `harness.py`, `proposer.py`, `signature.py` and `orchestrator/check.py`. I did not run any tests or ESBMC. Findings are ordered by severity; the first three are behavior changes, the rest are cleanups.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/properties/harness.py",
    "line": 521,
    "summary": "The cap is now applied whenever `_contradicts_cap` cannot read a literal floor, so a floor written as a macro, disjunction or relation is capped instead of left authoritative.",
    "failure_scenario": "A domain such as `n >= MIN_LEN` (macro above the cap), `n == 20 || n >= 16`, or `lo >= 5 && lo <= hi` with `hi` capped at 2 is not read as a floor above the cap. The harness then assumes `n <= 8` on top of it and the assumptions are jointly unsatisfiable. The verdict becomes VACUOUS (probe) where it used to be UNKNOWN, and a proposer candidate that was previously checkable is spent on a vacuous run. The docstring admits this for `hi - lo >= 20`, but the old `_leaves_room` was fail-closed for these shapes and the new code is fail-open."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/properties/harness.py",
    "line": 521,
    "summary": "`length_bounds` reports the cap even when the domain's own upper bound is already tighter, so the verdict is labelled `len<=max_len` when the real scope is smaller.",
    "failure_scenario": "Domain `n >= 1 && n <= 4` with max_len=8 now returns `length_bounds == (('n', 8),)` and emits a redundant `n <= 8` assume. Previously it returned `()`, meaning the domain bounded the length. A reader of the verdict sees `held up to k, len<=8` although the property was only checked for len<=4. Consumers can no longer tell a real narrowing of the user's bound (`n <= 100` to 8) from a redundant cap."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/properties/signature.py",
    "line": 241,
    "summary": "`scratch_param_names` re-implements the private `_is_scalar_backed` predicate from harness.py (`out and length.strip() == '1'`) in negated form.",
    "failure_scenario": "The renderer decides what to `malloc` with `_is_scalar_backed`, while the proposer and `renderability_reason` decide which outputs may take a non-NULL domain with `p.length.strip() != '1'`. If the scalar-backed rule ever changes (for example to accept a literal `1u` or `(1)`), the two drift. A domain on a buffer the harness never allocates would then be accepted, or one on a malloc'd buffer rejected. Make `is_scalar_backed` a `BufferParam` property and derive both from it."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/properties/proposer.py",
    "line": 484,
    "summary": "Adding scratch names to the allowed idents lets a domain consisting only of `tmp != NULL` pass the proposer's vacuous-domain check.",
    "failure_scenario": "The V5 check at lines 415-418 only requires that `set(pre_idents) - HARNESS_MACROS` is non-empty, and `tmp` now counts. The clause is true by construction because the harness already assumes `tmp != NULL`. A candidate whose only precondition is `tmp != NULL` is accepted as a real domain, although it constrains nothing."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/properties/harness.py",
    "line": 570,
    "summary": "`_length_floor` now parses hex literals but still reads a leading-zero literal as decimal, although C reads it as octal.",
    "failure_scenario": "`n >= 014` is 12 in C but is read as floor 14. With max_len=12 the floor is judged to exceed the cap, so the cap is wrongly suppressed and the length stays unbounded. The check reports UNKNOWN at every rung, which is the symptom #438 sets out to remove. The hex branch added here sits right beside the unfixed octal case."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/properties/harness.py",
    "line": 535,
    "summary": "`_conjuncts` and `_unwrap_parens` add a hand-rolled scanner that needs mutually recursive re-unwrapping.",
    "failure_scenario": "The code walks the text to split on `&&`, then recurses into each part, which unwraps parentheses again with another O(n) scan per level. Only `(` and `)` are tracked, not `|`, `&` or `?:`, so `n >= 16 & x` or a ternary is silently treated as one opaque conjunct. A single pass that tracks depth and splits once, or a shared top-level splitter (the codebase already has `references` and `identifiers` helpers over expressions), would be simpler to maintain."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/properties/test_harness.py",
    "line": 998,
    "summary": "New test comments narrate change history and issue numbers instead of explaining non-obvious code, against the global comment rule.",
    "failure_scenario": "~/.claude/CLAUDE.md says 'Use comments sparingly. Only comment complex code.' Comments such as '# #438: an upper bound above `max_len` (`n < 100`) used to suppress the cap and leave the length unbounded in practice (UNKNOWN at every rung)...', and the same style in test_proposer.py and test_harness_integration.py, describe past behavior. That history belongs in the commit message or PR."
  }
]
```
